     }
    ],
    "section": "Research method"
   },
   {
    "text": "The CCC reported that employees ranked policy non-compliance, mishandling confidential information, and bullying and harassment among the highest workplace corruption risks.",
    "cites": [
     {
      "url": "https://www.ccc.qld.gov.au/sites/default/files/Docs/Publications/CCC/CPS25-Public-Sector-Employee-Report.pdf",
      "document": "Queensland Public Sector Employees Report",
      "page": 14
     }
    ],
    "section": "Summary of key findings"
   },
   {
    "text": "The CCC reported that 55% said they had witnessed or suspected corruption in the preceding five years, and 55% of that group had not reported it.",
    "cites": [
     {
      "url": "https://www.ccc.qld.gov.au/sites/default/files/Docs/Publications/CCC/CPS25-Public-Sector-Employee-Report.pdf",
      "document": "Queensland Public Sector Employees Report",
      "page": 14
     }
    ],
    "section": "Summary of key findings"
   },
   {
    "text": "The CCC reported that 72% would report corruption, but only 33% expected meaningful action and 52% expected personal repercussions.",
    "cites": [
